                    datafile (it's in /plib/pgm).
                - Each class of data is then gotten from a function call
                - The raw data is in the /plib/data directory.  This directory's files are
                in whatever form is suitable for the main storage.  I'd prefer either
                plain text or JSON, as they are easy to manually edit.
                    - Plain text files can have comments in them that use '#" to mark the
                    lines.  This lets get.GetLines() get the needed data.
                - The data from /plib/data are not loaded until the function is called,
                meaning module loading is fast.  Some of the more common stuff may be
                loaded at runtime if that makes sense.
                - The function doing the loading can vet the data as necessary
                - This should help reduce the number of files in /plib and speed things up.
                It should also make maintenance easier.
        - Since constant.py is now working as a flexible constant variable container, it
          should be used by all scripts.  I'd like to standardize on the pattern
            - from constant import Constant
            - G = Constant()
            - g = Constant()
            - g.strict = False
            - Then G is a container for true readonly stuff and g is writable as needed.
              However, g can be set to readonly when desired, possibly eliminating later
              bugs when accidentally changing something.  Of course, either of them can
              be modified as needed using the context manager.
        - si.py can become a core module to deliver SI units, prefixes, and many
          conversion factors to other non-SI units
        - Standardize on 'import x' instead of 'from x import y'.  The reason is because
          then someone reading the code then knows where the relevant token is coming
          from, rather than having to figure it out -- which can sometimes be time
          co nsuming in large complicated code.  But it's OK in the code after the if
          __name__ == "__main__":  section.
        - A benefit of standardizing on the 'if 1:  # Header' in all files is that it
          allows an automated tool to inspect that each of the used "fields" is present.
          This in turn allows other tools to do code introspection and analysis.
        - Remove __all__ stuff:  this so easily gets out of date.  Figure out how to
          just remove the stuff that doesn't need to be in the global namespace.
        - Subdirectories: shop elec math sci util lang_or_text color
        - Gist in every python file
            - gist command
                - gist      List all files: gists on one line; regex gets subset
                - kw        List all cat keywords used; -v includes the files
                - test      List all test keywords used; -v includes the files
                - todo      List all todo items, sorted by priority (use num as regex)
        - Reference data:  a number of the files contain links to information sources
          (e.g., density.py).  These need to be checked on a periodic basis and flagged
          for when they go defunct.  A gist field could be added to handle this case.
        - Module usage statistics
