    - asciify.py
        - Goal:  'U | asciify -' should produce pure ASCII.  Still, it's going to be
          idiomatic and idiosyncratic because there have to be so many personal choices.
            - I won't even try converting all the emoji stuff out there
        - Vision:  the new codepoints entry can be used to modify asciify.py to allow it
          to turn arbitrary Unicode data (up to U+FFFF) into plain ASCII text in a
          uniform and readable manner.  This will be done by using the name attribute of
          the Unicode data to get an ASCII form, such as
            - ξ:  GREEK SMALL LETTER XI --> <xi>
            - ἀ:  GREEK SMALL LETTER ALPHA WITH PSILI --> <alpha+1f00>
                - This might become <alpha+1f00>, which shows that it's a lowercase
                  Greek letter and includes the codepoint number.
            - Ἀ:  GREEK CAPITAL LETTER ALPHA WITH PSILI
                - This might become <ALPHA+1f08>, which shows that it's a capital Greek
                  letter
            - ᱳ:   OL CHIKI LETTER LO --> <lo+1c73>
                - This would be the general pattern:  lower case unless "capital" in
                  name
            - This will require a function that can parse the name string
        - Add -a option which converts the text file to a 7-bit clean ASCII format.
          This is needed because my conversion choices were idiosyncratic to my own
          tastes and things like α β ɣ Δ are not converted.  These Unicode characters
          can be converted to things like <alpha>, <beta>, <gamma>, <DELTA>, etc.
        - The self-test for this feature doesn't need to check all letters, but it does
          need to show that it's 7-bit clean

