    - Serial stuff
        - It would be handy to write a hobbyist document that addresses the basics of
          serial communications.  This would revolve around the serial communications
          used on Arduinos.  There's a goodly bit of confusion around terms like UART,
          RS-232, and the voltage levels involved.  Another interesting part is learning
          to use an oscilloscope to decode a serial signal.

    - Test equipment
        - There are four basic instruments that get used a lot on the bench:  DMM,
          scope, power supply, function generator.  It would be handy to have a basic
          document that outlines the use of these four instruments.
        - Other instruments
            - LCR meter
            - DC load
            - Variac
            - Clamp-on ammeter
            - Analog meters
            - Shunts
            - Analog AC voltmeter
        - I'd discuss my background as an electrical hobbyist a bit.  The only "real"
          course in electrical stuff was a network theory course taken in college,
          typically a junior-level EE course that focused on solving circuit equations
          (typically differential equations) gotten from voltage & current laws and the
          time-dependent behavior, using things like Laplace and Fourier transforms.
          This was good background for theoretical stuff, but had no associated lab or
          practical stuff.  The same teacher invited me to sit in on his communications
          course a couple of years later and I took in about 2/3 of that before
          stopping (it was an excellent course and where I first used the HP 3400A RMS
          voltmeter).  Then it was "teach yourself".  25 years at HP let me get to know
          some very good EEs who helped me with things as needed and one lunch course
          taught by a local univerity teacher (EE for Non-EEs) was excellent.  But none
          of the course stuff looked much at the practical stuff, which had to be
          learned on your own.  Advice:  learn basic DC stuff really well, as much of it
          will "port" over to AC behavior too, particularly when RMS measurements are
          used.

