          they won't compare directly.  And if there's different indentation and
          linefeed breaks, a 'diff -w' won't work.
        - 'diff -w' partially works
        - A heuristic that could provide a guide
            - ASCIIfy the text
            - Change all punctuation to space characters except '.', "'", and '-'
            - Change all whitespace to space characters
            - Use dpstr.Tokenize to get a bunch of words
            - Remove all space characters
            - Contractions https://editorsmanual.com/
                - don’t didn’t isn’t wasn’t aren’t weren’t hasn’t haven’t hadn’t can’t
                  couldn’t shan’t shouldn’t won’t wouldn’t mightn’t mustn’t oughtn’t
                  needn’t could’ve should’ve would’ve might’ve must’ve I’m you’re she’s
                  he’s it’s we’re they’re I’ve you’ve we’ve they’ve I’ll you’ll he’ll
                  she’ll it’ll we’ll they’ll I’d you’d she’d he’d it’d we’d they’d
                  that’s that’ve that’d which’ve who’s who’re who’ve who’d who’ll what’s
                  what’re what’ll where’s where’d when’s why’s why’d how’s here’s
                  there’s there’ll there’d someone’s somebody’s one’s nobody’s
                  something’s nothing’s let’s ma’am o’clock 'tis
        - Figure out a scoring scheme that measures how close two documents are
            - Use rapidfuzz https://github.com/rapidfuzz/RapidFuzz.  This works very
              well and checked two copies of pnp that differed by perhaps 10 letters or
              so in 3.7 s and returned 99.99840298032908 (fuzzywuzzy only returned an
              integer, which is why I uninstalled it).
            - Quick heuristic
                - Replace all characters above 0x7f with spaces
                - Change all punctuation to space characters except '.', "'", and '-'
                - Change all whitespace to space characters
                - Change all control characters to space characters
                - Use fuzz metric to compare

