    - color.py
        - For Color() stuff and wavelength stuff
        - Color constructor
            - float
                - [0, 1]:  grays
                - [380, 780]:  wavelength of light
            - int
                - [0, 255]:  8-bit color palette
                - [380, 780]:  wavelength of light
            - Sequence
                - int(0xff, 0xff, 0xff) 24-bit color
                - float (x, y, z) on [0, 1]  24-bit color
            - String
                - #ffffff RGB
                - $ffffff HLS
                - @ffffff HSV
                - "255 135 0"   Tuple of ints
                - "25.5 135 0"  Tuple of floats (normalize to unit vector)
                - name  Search color name database
    - clr.py
        - Trm class for use with color in terminals
            - Internal stack with cpush() and cpop() for name definitions
            - Provides escape sequences
        - Color names
            - Styles:  define a set of default styles and you load them in the
                current instance with t.styles("name")
                - This allows for things like a Usage() style for writing
                    Usage() statements
            - 4-bit palettes could be useful for simple program use
    - Names
        - One & two letter
            - Current names:  blk:k blu:b brn:bn cyn:c den:dn grn:g gry:gy lav:la lil:ll
            lip:lp lwn:ln mag:m olv:ov orn:o pnk:pi pur:p red:r roy:ry sea:se sky:sk
            trq:tq vio:v wht:w yel:y
            - 4-bit names:  k:blk, b:blu, g:grn, c:cyn, r:red, m:mag, y:yel, w:wht
            - One letter:  add 0 or 1 to darken, 2 or 3 to lighten
        - 3 letter:  hxx where xx is hex:  use an 8-bit palette
    - Styles
        - Pass t.styles() a dict; the dict's names become attribute names with the
            associated formatting.  You add them with t.push(dict) and take them off
            with t.pop().  This lets you have a subroutine use its own set of colors,
            then let the calling routine resume its colors.
            - A nice way of doing this for e.g. a Usage() statement is to let you
                put the desired colors in Usage.clr = {}.  Then you call
                t.styles(Usage.clr) before producing output in the print() statements.
                Need to figure out a clever way of making print() know these styles.
                This could be done with a class and make it a context manager.
                - This could be a use case for two-letter color names:  bn, bk, wt
                    gr, bu, etc. with "l" for light.  Inside the function, these
                    colors would be gotten with {gr} and {no} to go back to normal.
                    This would be concise and readable: bn rd on yl gr bu vi gy wt cy
                    mg pk lp lv ll pu ry dn sy tq se ln ov
        - However, in a Usage() statement, 'e, b, n = t.purl, t.cynl, t.n' works
            nicely too and is simpler than defining a bunch of new structure.
            - 'e, b, n = t("purl cynl n".split())' could return these and this would
                be a compact and readable syntax; it wouldn't require much new code
        - Would it make sense to derive t from dict?  Then colors would always have
            to be gotten like t["name"], which is clumsy in f-strings.  Attribute
            access is cleaner syntactically.
        - Could a t instance also be a primitive text formatter?  This could have a
            method t.fmt(s) that would process the passed string to identify
            formatting commands and color style changes.
            - With a trigger string such as .., new colors could be put in text
                with commands like ..abc>, where abc is a defined style or color.  The
                > would be the termination character.  Or, it might be even simpler
                with esc being the start and esc being the end.
    - Rearchitect?
        - I think the overwhelming use of the color.py module is for getting
            colorized output to the terminal
        - Because of this, make color.py only provide the TRM/Trm/t thingies
        - t() can figure out a color from a decimal (implies a gray), a tuple
            of integers or floats, or a string like "#ffffff".  Today, it will
            virtually always be 24 bit color.
        - Put the other less-used stuff in e.g. colorother.py or somesuch.
        - Develop a module named regexdecorate.py so that its needed functionality
            is in one place and is well-tested and always works.
        - Another module can be colorstyle.py, which allows you to define styles.
            These can then be used to define attributes to t.  The t attribute holds
            the style data, which allows it to be updated dynamically.
        - Other modules color*.py define other related stuff
        - I very much like the 24 colornames I've chosen.  I think they can be
            tweaked a bit.  For example, vio, lav, lil, and pur are pretty close and
            might need some tweaks.  
            - I might define "primary" colors to be red, grn, blu, mag, cyn, and
                yel.  That's 6 colors, implying that I should have about 4 of each 
                type spread over the visible spectrum.  This will need to be shown
