/plib Typing xyzzyy

    AI for advice:  https://gemini.google.com/app/33bd3d7229385f0e 

    1.  Start simple:  in your .mypy.ini, set a permissive global floor so you can work
    on one module at a time without the rest of the project screaming at you:

        [mypy]
        # Only check files that have at least one type hint or a 'pass'
        check_untyped_defs = False
        # Don't error out if a function doesn't have hints yet
        disallow_untyped_defs = False
        # Stop the "noise" from libraries
        ignore_missing_imports = True

    2. Phase: The "Outside-In" Approach
        - Don't start refactoring the logic immediately. Start by defining the
          boundaries.
        - Identify your "Leaf" Modules: Find the low-level utility modules that don't
          depend on anything else. Type-hint these first.
        - Define Data Structures: If you have old dictionaries or tuples acting as
          records, convert them to TypedDict or Dataclasses. This provides the biggest
          "bang for your buck" in IDE auto-completion.
        - The "Follow the Data" Rule: Type-hint the function signatures (inputs and
          outputs) before you worry about the logic inside the function bodies.

    3. Use the "Follow the Error" Workflow
        - Instead of guessing where to start, use mypy as a scout:
            - Pick one core module you want to refactor.
            - Add # type: ignore to lines that are truly baffling or ancient logic you
              don't want to touch yet.
            - Run mypy only on that file: mypy my_module.py.
            - Fix the errors until it passes, then move to the next.

    4. Strategic Refactoring Tools
        - Since you're coalescing 140 modules, you're likely moving a lot of code.
        - Structural vs. Nominal Typing: Use Protocol (from the typing module) if you
          have different classes that share the same methods. It’s "duck typing" for
          mypy.
        - The Any Escape Hatch: If a piece of 20-year-old logic is too complex to
          type-hint today, just mark the variable as Any. It’s better to have 90% of
          your file type-checked than 0% because you got stuck on one tricky line.

    Starting with the "leaf" modules—those that don't depend on anything else—is the
    most tactically sound move. It creates a solid foundation of "known truths" that
    mypy can then use to validate the more complex modules that import them.

    Since you're diving into the core, here are three "pro-tips" for an architect
    working solo on a legacy codebase:

    1. The if TYPE_CHECKING: Guard

        - As you refactor, you might hit circular imports (Module A needs a type from B,
          but B imports A). This is common in older, organic codebases. You can solve
          this without changing your runtime logic:

            from typing import TYPE_CHECKING

            if TYPE_CHECKING:
                # This only runs during a mypy scan, not at runtime
                from .complex_module import HeavyClass

            def process_data(item: "HeavyClass") -> None:
                ...

        - Note: Using quotes around "HeavyClass" (forward reference) tells Python "don't
          look for this class until the function is actually called."

    2. Use TypeAlias for Domain Logic
        - After 30 years, you likely have specific data shapes (e.g., a coordinate
          system or a complex measurement). Instead of typing list[tuple[float, float,
          float]] everywhere, define it once: 

            from typing import TypeAlias

            # Now your intent is clear across all 140 modules
            Coordinate3D: TypeAlias = tuple[float, float, float]
            UncertainValue: TypeAlias = float  # Or a specific uncertainties type

    3. Embrace reveal_type()

        - This is the single best "hidden" feature of mypy. If you are ever unsure what
          mypy thinks a variable is, stick this in your code:

            reveal_type(my_variable)

        - When you run mypy, it will print a note: note: Revealed type is
          "builtins.list[mpmath.ctx_mp.mpf]". Just remember to delete it before actually
          running the script, as it's not a real Python function!  

    - A "Sanity Check" for your Progress
        - As you clean up these core modules, you might find that some are so small they
          should just be merged. A good rule of thumb: If a module is under 100 lines
          and only exists to support one other module, merge them. It reduces the
          "import tax" on your brain.
    - A Tip on Refactoring Structure
        - Since you mentioned you're coalescing 140 files, as you move code from an old
          file to a new one, type-hint the new file immediately. It is 10x easier to
          type-hint fresh code than to retroactively figure out what a variable was
          supposed to be in a file written in 1998.
